## Source map and behavior

- `wire.go`: strict known-field parsing, batch admission validation, decimal
  strings, canonical payload digest, wire/storage structs.
- `native.go`: one synchronous C wrapper per batch, calling only
  `rn_verify_v1`. C-owned source allocation and arena live through verify return;
  v1 item pointers refer to `rn_arena_data`. Go input/results have no retained
  native pointers. Native error-result sizes and digests are zeroed.
- `service.go`: four workers; FIFO pending queue; four native slots shared with
  evaluate calls; sequence-ordered committer; cancellation and drain state.
- `recovery.go`: schema/counter/index/digest/receipt validation and recovery of
  pending payloads and cancellation intents. Refuses incompatible or inconsistent
  databases without rebuilding them.
- `transport.go`: bounded newline framing, eight live clients, sequential
  handling per client, one output writer per socket, best-effort terminal events.
- `reuse/storage.go`: supplied open/get/WriteBatch wrapper, unchanged.
  `reuse/iterator.go` adds startup scanning through the same RocksDB C API.

One mutex serializes state and every RocksDB write, including the complete C
write call. This deliberately prevents cancellation acknowledgement in the gap
between a terminal decision and native `call_entry`. It also means status and
other transitions wait during synchronous writes and external write barriers.
Native encoding and socket writes do not hold this mutex. The committer releases
it between individual terminal writes.

Admissions persist full items, digest, index, sequence, and outstanding totals
before acknowledgement. Cancellation acknowledgement follows its synchronous
intent write. Queued cancellations can become ready immediately; running jobs
retain input and await native return. Completed jobs remain cancellable while
waiting behind an earlier sequence. Terminal batches atomically write the
receipt, remove pending/cancellation keys, release accounting, and advance the
checkpoint. ID indexes and receipts are retained.

Receipt notifications are armed only after the admission response has actually
been written, and sent only after durable terminal commitment. A full event
queue drops notifications. Socket writes have a five-second deadline; a stalled
client is disconnected without cancelling its jobs. Status remains the recovery
path. Draining waits for accepted jobs, evaluations, and active request replies.

Storage settings remain compression off, 128 MiB block cache, 64 MiB write buffer,
two write buffers, two background jobs, WAL enabled, sync enabled. All multi-key
transitions use WriteBatch. Native RocksDB LOCK enforces exclusive ownership.
Fresh initialization writes version and all scalar counters durably. A database
containing only schema version 1 is treated as fresh; otherwise missing counters
are an error.

Storage/acknowledged-job arena failures stop the process with diagnostics and
leave durable pending work for restart. A nonpersistent evaluate allocation/call
failure returns `execution_failure`. Per-item adapter statuses 0–4 are returned
as results; transaction parsing is entirely inside the supplied kernel.

## Validation and evidence

Tests cover exact/prefix native results and digest order, malformed and resource
statuses, request framing and validation, digest identity/conflicts, both
outstanding admission limits, durable cancellation recovery, completed work
waiting behind the head, four-worker dispatch/payload lifetime, concurrent
retries/cancellations, corrupt recovery rejection, and drain/reply ordering.

Integration tests exercise actual Unix sockets, notification order, eight-client
concurrency, exclusive datadir ownership, receipt restarts, concurrent retries and
cancellation, and three SIGKILL/recovery cycles per run. They read RocksDB only
after its writer stops and independently verify physical keys/accounting against
acknowledged histories. The suite runs against ordinary and race-instrumented
executables. Test artifacts and stderr are retained under `tmp/v1-e2e-*`.

See `EXPERIMENTS.md` for executed results, environment failures, the corrected
resource fixture, and evidence limits. Go race checks do not cover C/C++ races.
No native ASan, allocation/storage-failure injection, external barrier
qualification, power-loss evidence, or performance comparison is claimed.

Final `./test.sh` passed all eleven Go test families, Go race tests, the public
smoke check, and both ordinary/race integration suites (six final SIGKILL recovery
cycles total). Final integration artifacts are `tmp/v1-e2e-h0znxot2` and
`tmp/v1-e2e-ao4odbsg`.

## Ambiguities and choices retained for maintenance

- Supplied preparation prose still mentions unfrozen gates; no campaign-freeze
  manifest was supplied here. Implementation followed the explicit phase-start
  instruction. Candidate testing does not establish gate qualification.
- Reserved unknown fields are ignored. Duplicate object keys at request/item
  level are rejected; required known fields use exact names and string types.
- V1 accepts omitted or explicit `normal` priority and rejects other priorities.
- Invalid batch count or aggregate bytes return `invalid_request`. Per-item
  limits are passed to the native adapter, preserving status 2/3 distinctions.
- Existing identical retries remain `existing` during draining; new IDs and
  evaluations return `draining`. Retrying does not alter priority or ownership.
- Oversized request lines get `invalid_request` with an empty correlation, then
  the connection closes. Unterminated EOF fragments are discarded.
- Arena source-copy timing is undocumented; keeping the C source allocation
  alive until arena destruction avoids depending on it.
- Receipts/indexes are cached in memory and retained on disk without expiry for
  this bounded trial. Startup scans the full database. No unbounded-deployment
  memory or startup-time claim is made.

No production test hooks, barriers, alternate kernels, or injected schedulers are
present. Unit tests directly arrange internal states for deterministic policy
checks; integration tests use only the public socket protocol and process kills.

